# Slide decks — authoring doctrine

`context/instructions/slides.md` is the contract for authoring or revising a
slide deck: an ordered set of surfaces, each carrying one claim, read at a
distance in seconds. It is format-agnostic — it names the decisions, not a
tool. When the deck is written as markdown, read it together with
`context/instructions/slides-marp.md` (the dialect and deck document-management
contract) and `context/instructions/development.md` (the process contract:
agreement, library-first, simplicity, surgical changes). It names no rendering
tool: a deck is a document, and any tool that reads the chosen dialect can
display it.

## When this applies (no-op rule)

- Use it when the project authors or revises a slide deck. If it authors none,
  this file does not apply — say so and stop.
- The doctrine is about the *content and structure* of a deck, not the tooling.
  Choosing a renderer or writing code is a `development.md` decision
  (library-first), not a rule of this module.

## The work loop

OUTLINE → AGREE → WRITE → RENDER → LOOK → FIX.

1. **Outline** — one message per slide, in order, as bare sentences, before any
   slide markdown exists. This is a thinking step, not a typing step.
2. **Agree** — the outline *is* the argument. Settle it with the requester
   before writing slides: restructuring a deck is cheap here and expensive once
   slides exist.
3. **Write** — turn each agreed message into a slide and apply the rules below.
4. **Render** — produce the deck with the project's toolchain.
5. **Look** — open the rendered slides and read them as the audience will: at a
   distance, in seconds, one after another.
6. **Fix** — correct what the look reveals: overflow, weak contrast, a title
   that does not state its claim, a slide carrying two ideas.

OUTLINE and WRITE are never merged. Structure discovered while formatting is
structure invented while formatting, and a deck with an invented structure is
the failure this loop exists to prevent.

## One idea per slide

A slide makes one claim. A slide making two has none: the audience satisfies
itself with the first and stops reading. The check is mechanical — read the
title, read the body, and ask whether one sentence could summarise both. If not,
split the slide.

## An assertive title

The title states the claim, not the topic: "caching cut p99 by 60%", not
"Caching". A topic title makes the audience derive the point from the body,
which is the work the deck exists to do. The strongest test: read only the
titles, in order — the argument must be reconstructible from them alone. If it
is not, the argument is not in the deck yet.

## A diagram beats a bullet list

When what is being said is a relationship — a sequence, a hierarchy, a flow, a
state change, an architecture — draw it. A bullet list *describing* a diagram
makes the reader rebuild the diagram mentally, during the seconds they were
supposed to spend on the claim. Bullets are for enumerations, not for
structure.

## Budgets (defaults, not laws)

- About 5–6 lines of text on a slide.
- Code blocks of 10–15 lines; longer code belongs in a handout or an appendix.
- One diagram instead of three bullets.

These are defaults with a reason: a slide is read at a distance, in seconds,
often in a room lit by a bright window. Override them knowingly — a conference
talk and an internal review are different objects — and say that you did.

## Speaker notes are not optional

The slide carries what the audience reads; the notes carry what the presenter
says. A slide without notes forces the presenter to invent an argument at the
podium, and the invented argument is rarely better than the planned one. Write
the notes while the claim is fresh.

## Legible from the back of the room

- **Contrast first** — text that fails a contrast check fails the room. Check
  it; do not assume it.
- **Never colour alone** — pair colour with text, position or shape, or the
  deck fails anyone with a colour vision deficiency, a washed-out projector or
  a black-and-white printout.
- **A restrained palette** — 4–6 colours, defined once in a theme instead of
  applied slide by slide. A theme is also the cheapest way to retune a deck for
  a dark room or a second venue.
- **A fixed type scale** — set it in the theme, never per slide.

## No template slop

Reject the defaults that arrive with the tooling: the same
hero-plus-three-bullets layout on every slide, everything centred, a decorative
gradient standing in for hierarchy, an icon where a claim belongs, a stock
illustration standing in for content. A deck that looks like every other deck
says nothing about this one.

## Render and look

A deck is never presentable because its source reads well. Produce it, look at
it, fix what the look reveals, look again. Never claim a deck is ready without
having seen the rendered slides.

## Version-scoped engine contract

Whatever tool renders the decks, its directives, themes, layout names and
options are version-scoped, and its failure mode is usually a silently wrong
slide rather than an error. Check the installed version and read its `--help` or
its documentation before relying on a syntax detail; never write a directive or
an option from memory.

## Honesty rule

- Never claim a deck is presentable or accessible without having rendered it
  and looked at it.
- A budget breach is a stated choice, never a hidden one.
- A verification step that could not run is `expected`, never `passed`; say
  which claim is therefore unverified.

## Boundary

- Tool choice, install and any code defer to
  `context/instructions/development.md` (library-first, simplicity, surgical
  changes).
- Dialect syntax and deck document conventions defer to
  `context/instructions/slides-marp.md`.
- This module names no rendering tool and no exporter.
